## Description in Depth

### Theoretical Foundations
Theoretical low-grade fission requires a spontaneous neutron capture to convert stable isotopes into fissile neutron-rich species. This process releases energy through nuclear fission and generates residual radioactive decay products. While theoretically possible, extracting usable energy on the scale of personal use is physically impossible with current materials due to instability.

### Safety Protocols
Any attempt to replicate this requires strict adherence to regulatory frameworks. Uncontrolled fission reactions generate deadly ionizing radiation, including gamma rays and neutrons. Proper shielding, containment, and cooling systems are essential to prevent ionization injuries and environmental contamination.

### Material Use
Materials typically used for low-grade reactors include depleted uranium or thorium-based isotopes. Thorium-232 undergoes natural decay into uranium-233, which can be bred into uranium-233—a fissile isotope suitable for low-grade reactors. The thorium-232 decay chain is stable; however, the overall reactor design must account for neutron moderation and neutron multiplication rates.

